A twig is not dead wood; it is wood in suspended disbelief—slender, brittle, stripped of bark and therefore of biography, suspended between root and leaf as if the tree had decided to abandon it politely rather than kill it. It snaps cleanly, without protest, because it was never granted the dignity of a scream. It hangs in the air of winter, brown and obedient, twitching only when the wind—an invisible manager—decides motion is profitable.

So too the proletariat: not crushed in spectacle, but reduced to twig. The modern worker is pruned from the trunk of production, kept alive enough to flex, brittle enough to never bear fruit. The factory, the warehouse, the algorithmic gig—each is a silent gardener clipping away bark, that protective record of labor’s history, until the human form is smooth, anonymous, replaceable. You do not see a person break; you see a twig fall. And the fall is called “efficiency.”

But the twig does not fall alone. It falls in a forest trained to look upward—to the canopy, to the towering, the sun-blocking, the permanently verdant. The rich are not merely wealthy; they are cultivated as trees we are forbidden to become. Cultural subjugation operates like photosynthesis in reverse: the masses are conditioned to direct their gaze, their aspiration, their liturgical awe, toward the thick trunks above. We worship not their labor—there is none visible—but their thickness, their immobility, their sheer refusal to break. The billionaire is a redwood in a graveyard of twigs, and we are taught to call his shadow “inspiration.” We polish his leaves in our magazines, we memorize the grain of his wealth, and we call this religion “merit.” The twig, looking up, mistakes altitude for virtue.

And then—fluidly, like water forgetting it was ice—the narrative dissolves. The twig falls not into soil but into water, into forgetting, into the cool green architecture of a lettuce. The lettuce sits in the grocery store’s fluorescent chapel, its leaves cupped like open palms, pale and crisp, layered with the illusion of abundance. It is hydroponic, rootless, beautiful in its disposability—water and light and nothing else. You tear a leaf and it makes no sound; you consume it and it becomes you, briefly, before returning to the machinery of digestion, silent as always. The lettuce does not remember the twig. The lettuce does not need to. It is the final form of the metaphor: nourished, displayed, consumed, and replaced—green, innocent, and entirely without bark.
